---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../../../components/Hero.astro';
import LangSwitch from '../../../components/LangSwitch.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Privacy policy of the Pétanque Meet app | Ludiven Studio"
	description="Privacy policy of the Pétanque Meet mobile app: Google or Apple account, games published on the map, tournaments, advertising, moderation, GDPR rights."
	lang="en"
	alternates={{ fr: '/petanque-meet/confidentialite/', en: '/en/petanque-meet/confidentialite/' }}
>
	<div class="wrapper stack gap-12 legal">
		<div class="stack gap-8">
			<LangSwitch lang="en" links={[{ lang: 'fr', href: '/petanque-meet/confidentialite/' }]} />
			<Hero
				title="Privacy — Pétanque Meet"
				tagline="What you publish on the map is public. The rest is neither sold nor handed over."
			/>
		</div>

		<p class="updated">
			This policy covers <strong>the Pétanque Meet mobile app</strong>
			(<code>com.ludivenstudio.petanquemeet</code>) and the meet-up map it shares with the website
			(<a href="/en/petanque-meet/">ludiven-studio.fr/petanque-meet</a>). The rest of the website has its
			<a href="/confidentialite/">own policy</a>. Last updated: 11 November 2026.
		</p>
		<p class="updated">
			This is a courtesy translation. In case of discrepancy, the
			<a href="/petanque-meet/confidentialite/" hreflang="fr">original French version</a> prevails.
		</p>

		<div class="summary">
			<p><strong>The essentials:</strong></p>
			<ul>
				<li>Looking at the map needs <strong>no account</strong>. To publish, join or report a game, you sign in with <strong>Google or Apple</strong>.</li>
				<li>What you publish is <strong>visible to everyone</strong>: the first name you enter, the pitch, the time, and for a tournament the club's information. Your email address, however, is never shown.</li>
				<li>Your GPS location is used to centre the map and to compute distances, on your phone. <strong>It is never sent to us.</strong></li>
				<li>An <strong>advertising banner</strong>, served by Unity LevelPlay (ironSource Ads and Unity Ads), funds the app. The ad networks receive nothing <strong>without your consent</strong>, requested at first launch. No data is resold.</li>
				<li>You can <strong>delete your account</strong> at any time from the app.</li>
			</ul>
		</div>

		<section class="stack gap-2">
			<h2>1. Who is the data controller</h2>
			<p>Publisher of the app and data controller:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>Company name: <strong>Ludiven Studio</strong></li>
				<li>Legal form: EURL (French single-member limited company) with share capital of €1,000</li>
				<li>Registration: 999&nbsp;253&nbsp;586 R.C.S. Vienne — SIRET 999&nbsp;253&nbsp;586&nbsp;00013</li>
				<li>Registered office: 1166 route de la Ranche, 38440 Châtonnay, France</li>
				<li>Contact: <a href="mailto:contact@ludiven-studio.fr">contact@ludiven-studio.fr</a></li>
			</ul>
			<p>See also the <a href="/mentions-legales/">legal notice</a> (in French).</p>
		</section>

		<section class="stack gap-2">
			<h2>2. Your account</h2>
			<p>
				To take action (publish a game or a tournament, sign up, report), you sign in with
				<strong>Google</strong> (Android) or <strong>Apple</strong> (iPhone). We then receive an account
				identifier and your email address; with Apple, this may be the relay address that hides yours. We never
				receive your password. With Google, the sign-in service also keeps the name and photo of your Google
				profile: we do not show them and do not use them.
			</p>
			<p>
				This account is used to link your games and sign-ups to you, on all your devices, and to enforce the
				rules. Your email address is shown only to you, in the "Account and help" screen, and is never shown to
				other players.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="stack gap-2">
			<h2>3. What is public</h2>
			<p>
				The map can be viewed by anyone, in the app as on the website, without an account. The following is
				visible to everyone:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>For a game</strong>: the pitch (exact position of the pin and name of the place), the date and time, the format, the number of places, the <strong>organiser's first name</strong> and the <strong>first names of those signed up</strong>.</li>
				<li><strong>For a tournament</strong>: the club's name, the title, the place, the date, the category, the entry fee, the details, and, if they were provided, the <strong>phone number</strong> and the <strong>poster</strong> (once approved by the publisher).</li>
				<li>The <strong>tournaments you follow</strong> are visible to no one: the organiser only sees how many players follow theirs.</li>
				<li>An <strong>anonymous marker</strong> per account, which makes it possible to recognise that several listings come from the same organiser, without revealing who they are.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The first name is the only free text of a game. It does not need to be complete: a first name or a
				nickname is enough. Do not put a surname, a number or an address in it.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="stack gap-2">
			<h2>4. Data processed</h2>
			<div class="table-scroll">
				<table>
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th>Data</th>
							<th>Purpose</th>
							<th>Legal basis</th>
							<th>Recipient</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td>Account identifier and email address</td>
							<td>Signing you in, linking your games to you</td>
							<td>Performance of the contract (Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR)</td>
							<td>Supabase; Google or Apple for sign-in</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Games: pitch, time, format, places, organiser's first name</td>
							<td>Publishing the game on the map</td>
							<td>Performance of the contract</td>
							<td>Supabase; public</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Sign-ups: first name, number of places, role</td>
							<td>Joining a game</td>
							<td>Performance of the contract</td>
							<td>Supabase; public</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Tournaments: club, title, details, fee, optional phone number, optional poster</td>
							<td>Announcing a tournament</td>
							<td>Performance of the contract</td>
							<td>Supabase; public once the poster is approved</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Followed tournaments</td>
							<td>Finding a tournament in your agenda; giving the organiser a number of interested players</td>
							<td>Performance of the contract</td>
							<td>Supabase; the organiser only sees a total</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Coordinates of a new pitch</td>
							<td>Finding the name of the place and of the town</td>
							<td>Performance of the contract</td>
							<td>OpenStreetMap (Nominatim), called by our server</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Fingerprint of the IP address (see §6)</td>
							<td>Limiting abuse and spam</td>
							<td>Legitimate interest (Art. 6(1)(f))</td>
							<td>Supabase</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Reports: reason, your details for wrong information, identifier of the reporting account</td>
							<td>Moderation</td>
							<td>Legitimate interest</td>
							<td>Supabase; the publisher, by email via Resend</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>"This is my club" request: your message (name, role in the club, phone number), your account's email address</td>
							<td>Checking with the club that you represent it, then moving its tournaments to your account</td>
							<td>Performance of the contract</td>
							<td>Supabase; the publisher, by email via Resend</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Text of a place search; for komoot, also the centre of the map shown, rounded to about ten kilometres, and the app's language</td>
							<td>Finding a town or an address on the map</td>
							<td>Performance of the contract</td>
							<td>IGN (Géoplateforme) and komoot (Photon): each search goes to both</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Usage statistics, with no account and no identifier (see §8)</td>
							<td>Knowing which features are used and improving the app</td>
							<td>Legitimate interest</td>
							<td>Umami, on our server (Hetzner, Germany)</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td>Advertising identifier and other device identifiers, IP address, interactions with ads, usage time, technical information, purchases (declared by Unity Ads; the app offers none) (see §9)</td>
							<td>Showing, choosing and measuring the advertising banner, fighting fraud</td>
							<td>Your consent (Art. 6(1)(a)). Up to version 0.1.6: your consent for personalised ads, legitimate interest otherwise</td>
							<td>Unity Technologies and its subsidiary ironSource (Unity LevelPlay, ironSource Ads, Unity Ads); through their auctions, other ad buyers. Up to version 0.1.6: Google (AdMob) and its advertising partners</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
			<p>
				The app does <strong>not</strong> collect your contacts or your photos (apart from the poster you
				choose to send). Your GPS location is never sent to us (see §5). Your surname is only asked for in a
				"This is my club" request, and only the publisher sees it. Your phone's advertising identifier is only
				read by the ironSource and Unity Ads advertising modules, and only with your consent (see §9): we do
				not receive it.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="stack gap-2">
			<h2>5. Your location</h2>
			<p>
				The "Near me" button asks for access to your location <strong>only when you tap it</strong>. The app
				reads one position, centres the map and computes the distances to the games, then stops location.
				This position <strong>is never sent to us</strong>.
			</p>
			<p>
				When you search for a place, komoot receives the centre of the map shown, <strong>rounded to about ten
				kilometres</strong>, to suggest nearby places first. Right after "Near me", this centre is close to
				your position: komoot therefore only knows its region.
			</p>
			<p>
				What we receive is the location of the <strong>pitch</strong> you choose on the map for a game or a
				tournament, never that of your device.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="stack gap-2">
			<h2>6. Fighting abuse</h2>
			<p>
				The service counts, per day, the listings, sign-ups and reports of each account and each connection.
				Your IP address is <strong>never stored</strong>: it is turned into a non-reversible fingerprint, mixed
				with a secret known only to the server. Without this secret, the fingerprint cannot be traced back to
				an address.
			</p>
			<p>
				The texts entered go through an automatic filter (links, offensive words). A listing reported by
				several players is hidden while the publisher checks it.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="stack gap-2">
			<h2>7. Reports and moderation</h2>
			<p>
				You can report a game or a tournament (offensive first name, fake listing, unsuitable poster, nobody
				came). Your report is <strong>anonymous towards other players</strong>: no one knows who made it. It
				is, however, recorded with your account identifier, to prevent serial reporting.
			</p>
			<p>
				The publisher is alerted by email and sees the listing, the first names, the reason, your details for
				wrong information and the number of reports, never the identity of who reported. They can restore the
				listing, remove it or suspend the offending account. For a suspended account, only the technical
				identifier is kept, so that the suspension holds.
			</p>
			<p>
				Tournament posters stay private until the publisher approves them. A rejected poster is erased at
				once.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>"This is my club".</strong> To take over your club's tournaments imported from a committee
				calendar, you give your name, your role in the club and a phone number. The publisher receives this
				message by email with your account's address, checks with the club, then replies to you by email.
				No one else sees it.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="stack gap-2">
			<h2>8. Usage statistics</h2>
			<p>
				The app sends to <strong>Umami</strong>, a cookie-free statistics tool installed on our own server, the
				name of the actions taken (opening the app, opening a game, signing up, publishing, signing in,
				sharing, reporting, seeing or tapping one of our house ads, with the name of the app shown…), together
				with the platform, the app and system version, the language, the screen size and the phone model.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>No</strong> first name, account identifier, email address, GPS location, search text or listing
				identifier is included. These statistics are linked neither to your account nor to your identity.
			</p>
			<p>
				Like any web service, Umami sees your phone's IP address. It does not keep it, but infers from it the
				country, the region and the city. To count visitors, it groups the actions made from the same
				connection and the same phone model, for one calendar month at most.
			</p>
			<p>
				This server is rented from <strong>Hetzner Online GmbH</strong>, which hosts it in Germany. Each of the
				studio's apps has its own separate statistics there, and no one else receives them. Versions of the app
				that have not yet been updated still send them to Umami Cloud (Umami Software, Inc., United States).
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="stack gap-2">
			<h2>9. Advertising</h2>
			<p>
				The app shows an <strong>advertising banner</strong> at the top of the screen. It funds the service,
				which stays free, and it is served by <strong>Unity LevelPlay</strong> with the
				<strong>ironSource Ads</strong> and <strong>Unity Ads</strong> networks (Unity Technologies and its
				subsidiary ironSource). When no ad is available, or if you refuse advertising, the banner shows our
				other apps instead, signed "Ludiven Studio". It shows our pétanque apps first; on Android, when they
				are all already installed on your phone, it may show one of our games. To know this, the app checks
				which of ours are installed there, and this information stays on your phone. Tapping the banner opens
				the store page of the app shown, with a note saying that you come from Pétanque Meet. The app downloads
				the list of these apps and their images from this website, sending nothing other than the request
				itself. Nothing else is sent, apart from the fact that one of these apps was shown or tapped (see §8).
				Up to version 0.1.6, this banner only presents Pétanque Scan.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Your consent.</strong> At first launch, the app asks whether you accept advertising from these
				partners, with two identical buttons, "Accept" and "Refuse". Your choice stays on your phone and you are
				asked again after 6 months. <strong>If you refuse, no advertising module is started</strong> and
				nothing is sent to them: only our banner for our other apps is shown. You can change your mind at any
				time: "Account and help" → "Ad choices". If you withdraw your consent, our partners' banner disappears
				at once and the app reports the withdrawal to Unity LevelPlay. On iPhone, after an "Accept", the app
				also asks for Apple's tracking permission; without it, the iPhone's advertising identifier is not used
				and the ads are not personalised.
			</p>
			<p>
				With your consent, to show ads, choose them according to your interests, measure them and fight fraud,
				the ironSource and Unity Ads modules store and read information on your phone. They receive its
				<strong>advertising identifier</strong> and other device identifiers, your <strong>IP address</strong>
				(which gives an approximate location, at city level), your interactions with the ads, how long you use
				the app, technical information (model, language, connection type, performance) and, according to Unity
				Ads, your purchases (the app offers none). Their auctions may pass the ad request on to other ad
				buyers. We pass on to them neither your account, nor your email address, nor your first name, nor your
				games.
			</p>
			<p>
				Versions of the app up to 0.1.6 still request their ads from <strong>Google AdMob</strong>. At each
				launch they check Google's consent form, which then sees your phone's IP address; without your
				consent, their ads are not personalised.
			</p>
			<p>
				You can also reset or delete the advertising identifier in your phone's settings. To learn more, and
				for ironSource's and Unity Ads' partners:
				<a href="https://unity.com/legal/game-player-and-app-user-privacy-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unity's
				privacy policy</a>, which covers Unity Ads, LevelPlay and ironSource. For versions up to 0.1.6: the
				<a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy?hl=en" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Privacy
				Policy</a>.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="stack gap-2">
			<h2>10. What is stored on your phone</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>your sign-in session and your email address;</li>
				<li>the first name you entered, the games you joined, the places you saved, the last spot shown on the map;</li>
				<li>your preferences: language, theme, hidden organisers, messages already seen;</li>
				<li>your choice about advertising and, if you accepted it, the data of the ironSource and Unity Ads advertising modules (see §9);</li>
				<li>a cache of map tiles and posters, for faster display.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				All of this stays on the device and disappears when you uninstall the app.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Reminders.</strong> If you accept it, the app schedules a reminder on your phone one hour
				before your games. These are local notifications: no server sends you a message.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="stack gap-2">
			<h2>11. Recipients and transfers outside the European Union</h2>
			<p>
				Your data is neither sold nor handed over. It is only processed by these providers:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>Supabase</strong> — database, accounts and poster storage. Data hosted in the European Union (Ireland).</li>
				<li><strong>Google</strong> and <strong>Apple</strong> — signing in to your account and distribution of the app.</li>
				<li><strong>Unity Technologies</strong> and its subsidiary <strong>ironSource</strong> — the advertising banner, with your consent (Unity LevelPlay, ironSource Ads, Unity Ads); their auctions may pass the ad request on to other ad buyers (see §9).</li>
				<li><strong>Google (AdMob)</strong> — the advertising banner and its consent form, in versions of the app up to 0.1.6.</li>
				<li><strong>BKG</strong> (German Federal Agency for Cartography and Geodesy) — map tiles (TopPlusOpen). As for any website, its servers see your phone's IP address.</li>
				<li><strong>IGN</strong> (French National Institute of Geographic and Forest Information) — address search: each search is sent to it. Map tiles of the first versions of the app. Its servers also see your phone's IP address.</li>
				<li><strong>komoot</strong> (Photon service, Germany) — worldwide place search: each search is sent to it, with the centre of the map shown rounded to about ten kilometres and the app's language.</li>
				<li><strong>OpenStreetMap Foundation</strong> — name of a new pitch (Nominatim service). Only the pitch's coordinates are sent to it, by our server.</li>
				<li><strong>Resend</strong> — sending moderation emails and replies to "This is my club" requests.</li>
				<li><strong>Hetzner Online GmbH</strong> (Germany) — hosting of our usage statistics server (see §8). Versions of the app not yet updated still send these statistics to <strong>Umami Software, Inc.</strong> (Umami Cloud).</li>
				<li><strong>GitHub</strong> (GitHub Pages) — hosting of this website, where the app checks whether there is a new version and reads the list of our house ads (see §9). As for any website, its servers see your phone's IP address.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The "Directions" button opens Google Maps or Apple Maps with the pitch's address. The "Share" button
				passes a link to the listing to the app you choose. Their use is then governed by these services.
			</p>
			<p>
				Some providers are US companies. These transfers are governed by the mechanisms of Chapter V of the
				GDPR: standard contractual clauses and, for the providers concerned, certification under the
				<em>EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework</em>.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="stack gap-2">
			<h2>12. Retention periods</h2>
			{/* TODO legal: the server has no purge job yet. These durations need one before release. */}
			<ul>
				<li><strong>Games and sign-ups</strong>: removed from the map as soon as they are over or cancelled, then erased <strong>12 months</strong> after their date.</li>
				<li><strong>Tournaments</strong>: removed from the map the next day, erased 12 months after their date. The poster is erased one week after the tournament. Following a tournament is erased once the tournament is over, at the latest with it.</li>
				<li><strong>Reports</strong>: erased once dealt with, and at the latest 12 months later.</li>
				<li><strong>"This is my club" requests</strong>: kept with your account, erased when you delete it.</li>
				<li><strong>Anti-abuse counters</strong>: erased after 30 days.</li>
				<li><strong>Account suspension</strong>: the technical identifier is kept for as long as the suspension lasts.</li>
				<li><strong>Usage statistics</strong>: kept for <strong>24 months</strong> on our server, then erased; its backups, 14 days. Those Umami Cloud still receives from versions not yet updated are erased there after 6 months.</li>
				<li><strong>Your choice about advertising</strong>: 6 months on your phone, then you are asked again.</li>
				<li><strong>Advertising data</strong>: according to the rules of Unity and ironSource (see §9).</li>
				<li><strong>Account</strong>: until you delete it.</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="stack gap-2">
			<h2>13. Deleting your account</h2>
			<p>
				In the app: <strong>"Account and help" → "Delete my account"</strong>. It is immediate and final:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>your account and your email address are erased;</li>
				<li>your upcoming games are cancelled (those signed up see it) and your first name is replaced by "Deleted account" on all your games;</li>
				<li>your sign-ups, your tournaments, their posters, the tournaments you follow, your reports and your "This is my club" requests are erased;</li>
				<li>on iPhone, the app asks you to sign in with Apple again, to remove Pétanque Meet's access to your Apple ID.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Your account is a Ludiven Studio account: the same one is used in our other apps. Deleting it also
				erases what they have saved for it (saves).
			</p>
			<p>
				Your past games stay in the database for a while, without a first name, linked to a technical
				identifier that no longer points to any account. They then follow the periods in §12.
			</p>
			<p>
				You can also ask for deletion by email at
				<a href="mailto:contact@ludiven-studio.fr">contact@ludiven-studio.fr</a>, from your account's address.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="stack gap-2">
			<h2>14. Your rights</h2>
			<p>
				Under the GDPR, you have the right of access, rectification, erasure, restriction, objection and
				portability. To exercise them, or to have a listing, a first name or a sign-up removed, write to
				<a href="mailto:contact@ludiven-studio.fr">contact@ludiven-studio.fr</a>. We reply within one month.
			</p>
			<p>
				If you believe your rights are not being respected, you can lodge a complaint with the French data
				protection authority, the CNIL (<a href="https://www.cnil.fr" target="_blank" rel="noopener">www.cnil.fr</a>),
				3 place de Fontenoy, TSA 80715, 75334 Paris Cedex 07.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="stack gap-2">
			<h2>15. Minors</h2>
			<p>
				The app is used to meet other players in real life. It is meant for people aged <strong>15 and
				over</strong>. A minor must have their parents' consent, and should not go alone to a game with
				strangers. If you are a parent and notice that a child has created an account, write to us: we will
				delete it.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="stack gap-2">
			<h2>16. Security</h2>
			<p>
				All exchanges are encrypted (HTTPS). The map's data can only be accessed by our server: no direct
				access to the database is open, not even for reading. Your session is kept in the app's storage on
				your phone.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="stack gap-2">
			<h2>17. Changes</h2>
			<p>
				This policy may change with the app. The date of the last update appears at the top of the page. In
				the event of a significant change, the app will let you know.
			</p>
			<p>See also the <a href="/en/petanque-meet/cgu/">app's terms of use</a>.</p>
		</section>
	</div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
